object straight track A

  regard the complete object as one train track
  orient the track from west to east
  measure geometry in millimetres

  centreline goes from (20, 50) to (180, 50)
  gauge is 20

  rail left is 10 to the left of the oriented centreline
  rail right is 10 to the right of the oriented centreline

  sleeper 01 crosses the centreline at x 25 and spans 15 on each side
  sleeper 02 crosses the centreline at x 45 and spans 15 on each side
  sleeper 03 crosses the centreline at x 65 and spans 15 on each side
  sleeper 04 crosses the centreline at x 85 and spans 15 on each side
  sleeper 05 crosses the centreline at x 105 and spans 15 on each side
  sleeper 06 crosses the centreline at x 125 and spans 15 on each side
  sleeper 07 crosses the centreline at x 145 and spans 15 on each side
  sleeper 08 crosses the centreline at x 165 and spans 15 on each side

  transform all rails and sleepers together when the track is moved,
  rotated, reflected, or scaled


realization pen drawing

  draw straight track A

  using a pen
  assuming a flat planar work surface
  once per semantic stroke
  restricted to straight working strokes in this first example

  traverse rail left from west to east
  traverse rail right from east to west
  traverse sleepers in increasing x
  alternate sleeper direction south-to-north then north-to-south

  make sure every rail and sleeper has exactly one working traversal
  make sure every repositioning move occurs with the pen raised
  make sure every working motion names the semantic stroke it realizes
  make sure the output still identifies the complete object as straight track A


realization material cutting

  cut the geometry of straight track A into plate

  using a machine-specific milling adapter
  assuming the stock, tool, work origin, and safe height have been verified
  progressively deeper per semantic stroke
  restricted to depth increments accepted by the machine-specific process plan
  back and forth by alternating the direction of successive depth passes

  make sure every semantic stroke reaches the requested final depth
  make sure rapid travel occurs only at the verified safe height
  make sure every depth pass maps back to its rail or sleeper

  do not emit runnable machine code until the adapter supplies machine limits,
  tool data, feeds, speeds, origin, clearance, and safety checks


inspection requests the language should support

  show how straight track A becomes ten semantic strokes
  show how rail left becomes geometry
  show how the ten strokes are ordered for the pen
  show how once per semantic stroke was checked
  show which G-code motions realize sleeper 04

  rotate straight track A by 30 degrees
  but keep the same pen-traversal policy

  go over both rails twice
  but each sleeper once

  replace sleeper 04 only
  without rebuilding unrelated parts of the track